Emit a fixed IPA module signing key for reproducible builds

libcamera generates a fresh random 2048-bit RSA key at build time
(src/meson.build custom_target ipa-priv-key), compiles the public key
into libcamera.so (ipa_pub_key.cpp) and installs a signature next to
each IPA module (ipa_rpi_vc4.so.sign). Two rootfs files therefore
change on every build, breaking byte-for-byte reproducibility. There
is no meson option to disable signing (it is gated on openssl.found())
and unsigned IPAs pay an IPC-isolation penalty, so instead pin the key:
RSA PKCS#1 v1.5 signing is deterministic, making both files stable.

The key is deliberately public. It asserts only "this IPA belongs to
this build of libcamera"; reproducible builds require every builder to
sign with the same key.

--- a/utils/gen-ipa-priv-key.sh
+++ b/utils/gen-ipa-priv-key.sh
@@ -1,11 +1,46 @@
 #!/bin/sh
 # SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
 # Copyright (C) 2020, Google Inc.
 #
 # Author: Laurent Pinchart <laurent.pinchart@ideasonboard.com>
 #
 # Generate an RSA private key to sign IPA modules
-
-key="$1"
-
-openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -out "${key}" -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048
+#
+# SeedSigner OS: emit a fixed key instead of generating a random one, so the
+# public key compiled into libcamera.so and the ipa_*.so.sign signature are
+# byte-identical on every build (reproducible images). The signature is not a
+# trust boundary: it only tells libcamera the IPA came from the same build, so
+# it may run in-process instead of isolated over IPC.
+
+key="$1"
+
+cat > "${key}" <<'EOF'
